# ==== HEADER-COLON ANCHOR · multi-line container header (AXIS D, struct cell)
#
# ⚠ INPUT→OUTPUT, not a fixpoint — see the `.expected` companion. `gg fmt`
# REJOINS a wrapped header, so the wrapped spelling exists only on pass one;
# pass two is a fixpoint on the rejoined form.
#
# A container's header-line trailing comment used to be anchored on the
# container NAME. On a MULTI-LINE header the name is on an EARLIER source line,
# so the same-line test correctly rejected the comment — and it then fell to
# the body loop and came out as a LEADING comment of the first field, i.e.
# documenting `T x`. The anchor is the header's `:`, the one position that is
# on the header's LAST source line whatever the header's shape.
#
# RED, observed on the pre-fix build: the comment was emitted on its own line
# at body indent, above the struct's only field, instead of staying on the
# rejoined header line.
#
# ⚠ NO LINE OF THIS HEADER MAY SPELL the cell's marker, or the rejoined header
# text the assertion checks for. The test finds its cell by searching the
# formatted output for the marker and taking the FIRST match, so a header
# mention shadows the real cell — and a header comment line can never satisfy
# the positional half, so the test would be stuck RED.
struct S[T]:    # multi-line header trailing
    T x

int main():
    return 0
